        <article class="paper-content" id="paper-body">
          <section id="abstract" class="paper-abstract">
            <p>
              <strong>Abstract.</strong> Biologically inspired mechanisms such as hard-isolated parallel processing streams and local synaptic plasticity are widely considered incompatible with modern GPU accelerators compared to end-to-end global backpropagation (Global BP). Across five falsification-driven research iterations (v1&ndash;v5), we refute this assumption both formally in <strong>Lean 4</strong> and empirically on <strong>Kaggle NVIDIA T4&times;2 silicon</strong>. We introduce <strong>SPRINT-LP</strong> (<em>Stream-Packed Residual Information-Preserving Native Tensor Local Plasticity</em>), uniting pure cuBLAS batched GEMM stream packing (<code>torch.baddbmm</code>), whole-step <code>torch.cuda.CUDAGraph</code> capture with static FP16 scaling and fused AdamW, and a bio-faithful <em>Shared V1/V2 Stem + Late Bifurcation + Deep-MAN++</em> topology.
            </p>
            <p>
              Our experiments establish four decisive results: (1) on a <strong>single GPU with 100% identical parameters</strong> (<code>L = 12, M = 3, d = 128</code>), increasing local partitioning from <code>K = 1</code> (Global BP) to <code>K = 6</code> reduces peak activation VRAM by <strong>79.0%</strong> (<code>758.1 &rarr; 159.2 MB</code>) while running <strong>11.5% faster</strong> (<code>54.71 &rarr; 48.43 ms/step</code>) via L2 cache locality; (2) at matched representation width (Iso-Width <code>D = 768</code>), <code>M = 4</code> parallel streams achieve <strong>0.79&times; latency</strong> and <strong>1.28&times; energy efficiency</strong> (up to <strong>0.73&times; latency</strong> and <strong>1.39&times; samples/Joule</strong> at <code>d = 256</code>); (3) on <strong>2-GPU Kaggle T4&times;2</strong> with identical parameters, our <em>Zero-Bubble Async Local Pipeline</em> delivers a <strong>2.47&times; real wall-clock speedup</strong> (<code>16.96</code> vs. <code>41.82 ms/step</code>); and (4) on parameter-matched natural image multi-prediction (<code>AffCIFAR-10 v2</code>, ~1.26M vs. 1.31M parameters), live 2-GPU async training incurs <strong>zero accuracy degradation</strong> (<code>77.58%</code> vs. <code>77.19%</code> on 1 GPU), <strong>outperforms Global BP on variant pose prediction</strong> (<code>+0.325</code> vs. <code>+0.318</code> R<sup>2</sup>; <code>15.3&deg;</code> vs. <code>16.2&deg;</code> angular MAE), and achieves clean stream orthogonality (<code>CKA = 0.057</code>).
            </p>
          </section>

          <h2 id="sec1">1. Introduction and Five-Iteration Falsification Trajectory</h2>
          <p>
            Modern deep learning relies predominantly on homogeneous backbones trained end-to-end with global backpropagation. When forced to optimize conflicting <em>multi-prediction</em> objectives simultaneously, such as class-level <em>invariance</em> alongside continuous geometric pose <em>variance</em>, shared parameters suffer destructive gradient interference (<code>cos(&nabla;L<sub>inv</sub>, &nabla;L<sub>var</sub>) &lt; 0</code>) <a href="#ref1">[1]</a> and feature superposition. In contrast, the primate visual cortex shares early orientation and contour processing in V1/V2 before bifurcating into anatomically segregated <strong>Ventral</strong> ("what", invariant) and <strong>Dorsal</strong> ("where/how", variant) streams coordinated via lateral modulation <a href="#ref2">[2]</a> <a href="#ref3">[3]</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Translating parallel streams and local plasticity onto silicon GPUs has historically failed wall-clock and memory criteria due to four hardware mismatches: (i) Python stream loops or narrow grouped convolutions (<code>groups=M</code>) causing cuDNN kernel fragmentation; (ii) iterative settling loops (20&ndash;50&times; slower) in energy-based predictive coding; (iii) heavy pixel-reconstruction auxiliary decoders <a href="#ref4">[4]</a> that erase activation VRAM savings; and (iv) deferred global <code>.backward()</code> calls or per-block <code>GradScaler</code> host-device synchronizations.
          </p>
          <p>
            Rather than relying on unverified intuition, the Enemites Research Team executed five consecutive falsification-first empirical iterations (v1&ndash;v5) on Kaggle NVIDIA T4 GPUs (Turing <code>sm_75</code>, 40 SMs, 65 TFLOPS FP16 Tensor Core, 320 GB/s GDDR6, 70 W TDP) <a href="#ref10">[10]</a>, rejecting every component that failed strict hardware or learning kill criteria (Table 1).
          </p>

          <p class="paper-table-caption"><strong>Table 1:</strong> Five-iteration falsification trajectory on Kaggle NVIDIA T4 / T4&times;2 silicon.</p>
