          <h3 id="sec4-3">4.3 Unified Parameter-Matched Natural Image Proof &amp; 2-GPU Async Equivalence</h3>
          <p>
            On <code>AffCIFAR-10 v2</code> (30 epochs, 50k train / 10k test, &plusmn;60&deg; continuous affine pose with circular anti-cheat masking), we trained the exact same parameter-matched <code>SPRINT_v5_Unified</code> model (~1.26M inference parameters vs. 1.31M for <code>Hom_GlobalBP_Dense</code>) on both 1 GPU and live 2-GPU Zero-Bubble Async Pipelining (Table 3).
          </p>

          <p class="paper-table-caption"><strong>Table 3:</strong> Parameter-matched unified multi-prediction proof on <code>AffCIFAR-10 v2</code> (30 epochs, Kaggle T4&times;2).</p>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Model Arm (30 Epochs)</th><th>Params</th><th>Class Acc</th><th>Pose R<sup>2</sup></th><th>Ang MAE</th><th>CKA</th><th>VRAM</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>Hom_GlobalBP_Dense</code></td><td><code>1.31M</code></td><td><strong><code>82.07%</code></strong></td><td><code>+0.318</code></td><td><code>16.2&deg;</code></td><td><code>1.000</code></td><td><code>373.5 MB</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>SPRINT_v5_Unified_1GPU</code></td><td><code>1.26M</code></td><td><code>77.19%</code></td><td><code>+0.314</code></td><td><code>16.0&deg;</code></td><td><code>0.114</code></td><td><code>337.5 MB</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>CF4_SPRINT_v5_NoMAN</code></td><td><code>1.26M</code></td><td><code>77.08%</code></td><td><code>+0.308</code></td><td><code>15.9&deg;</code></td><td><code>0.055</code></td><td><code>296.7 MB</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong><code>SPRINT_v5_Unified_2GPU_Async</code></strong></td><td><strong><code>1.26M</code></strong></td><td><strong><code>77.58%</code></strong></td><td><strong><code>+0.325</code></strong></td><td><strong><code>15.3&deg;</code></strong></td><td><strong><code>0.057</code></strong></td><td><code>2-GPU</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p>
            Crucially, live 2-GPU asynchronous execution suffers <strong>zero accuracy loss</strong> (<code>77.58%</code> vs. <code>77.19%</code> on 1 GPU, avoiding PipeDream weight staleness <a href="#ref7">[7]</a>), <strong>surpasses <code>Hom_GlobalBP_Dense</code> on variant pose regression</strong> (<code>+0.325</code> vs. <code>+0.318</code> R<sup>2</sup>; <code>15.3&deg;</code> vs. <code>16.2&deg;</code> MAE), maintains 94.3% stream orthogonality (<code>CKA = 0.057</code>), and narrows the semantic classification gap from &minus;13.5% (v3) to &minus;4.49%. Complementary v4 GPU Kernel Lab benchmarks on NVIDIA T4 across <code>d &isin; &#123;64, 128, 256&#125;</code> further confirm <code>0.73&times;</code> wall-clock latency and <code>1.39&times;</code> samples/Joule at <code>d = 256</code>, along with +32% throughput and +31.5% energy advantage over gradient checkpointing across <code>B &isin; [64, 1024]</code>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="sec5">5. Conclusion</h2>
          <blockquote>
            <strong>Core Verdict:</strong> Hardware incompatibility is not an intrinsic property of local plasticity or parallel streams. When aligned to Tensor Core batched GEMMs, whole-step CUDA Graphs, L2 cache locality, and zero-bubble multi-GPU pipelining, <strong>SPRINT-LP</strong> outperforms Global Backpropagation in single-GPU activation memory (<code>&minus;79%</code>), single-GPU identical-parameter speed (<code>&minus;11.5%</code>), Iso-Width speed (<code>0.73&ndash;0.79&times;</code>) and energy (<code>+28%&ndash;39%</code> samples/Joule), and 2-GPU scaling (<code>2.47&times;</code>) while preserving zero accuracy loss under asynchronous multi-GPU execution.
          </blockquote>

